Description: Bande desktop environment
 The Bande desktop: a pure Wayland desktop with its own compositor, shell,
 session services, login window and applications, on Debian testing.
 .
 It brings a way to log in: the Bande login window under greetd, or the
 display manager the system already has. With recommends, the login
 window comes along anyway, and debconf asks which display manager to
 start; "dpkg-reconfigure greetd" asks again later. Sound runs on PipeWire.
 .
 It recommends the Bande APT repository, through which the system follows
 every Bande release and every Bande app. Passwords apps and networks
 keep live in the Bande keychain, unlocked when you log in. Other apps,
 such as a browser, and printing come from Software and Settings.
 .
 So that running out of memory never freezes the computer, it recommends
 compressed swap in memory (zram, sized like Fedora's and compressed with
 zstd) and systemd-oomd, and makes the apps' slice the place where
 systemd-oomd closes an app when memory stays under pressure, so the
 compositor and the session services keep running. It keeps up to 640 MB
 of the session (compositor, shell and session services) out of reclaim,
 through MemoryLow on user.slice, user-*.slice and user@.service, so the
 screen stays responsive while apps thrash, and caps all apps together at
 a quarter of the system's task limit.
 .
 Updates install while the computer runs. After each dpkg run, it restarts
 the system services that still run replaced code, except the login
 manager, the buses, logind, polkit, PackageKit and networking; Software
 Update in Settings handles the session and the apps.
